WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.760
Unsafe command construction is one of the easiest ways to turn a useful script into a security problem.

2
00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:12.184
Building a command as a string and then executing it can allow input to become code.

3
00:00:12.184 --> 00:00:18.473
Invoke-Expression is especially risky because it evaluates constructed text as PowerShell.

4
00:00:18.473 --> 00:00:28.299
In most administrative scripts, there is a safer way to call the command directly, pass arguments as parameters, and let PowerShell handle binding.

5
00:00:28.299 --> 00:00:31.600
Safe parameter passing is the preferred pattern.

6
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:40.168
Instead of concatenating switches and values into a command string, call the cmdlet or function directly with named parameters.

7
00:00:40.168 --> 00:00:49.994
Splatting is useful when parameters are dynamic or numerous: a hashtable can collect validated values and pass them to a command in a structured way.

8
00:00:49.994 --> 00:00:54.396
This keeps data as data instead of turning it into executable text.

9
00:00:54.396 --> 00:00:59.506
Working with objects is usually safer than scraping and rebuilding raw text.

10
00:00:59.506 --> 00:01:07.209
PowerShell pipelines pass rich objects between cmdlets, which reduces parsing mistakes and makes intent clearer.

11
00:01:07.209 --> 00:01:20.651
When a script must call an external tool, review how arguments are constructed, how paths are quoted, how exit codes are handled, and whether user-controlled values can change the meaning of the command.

12
00:01:20.651 --> 00:01:23.874
Treat external process execution as a boundary.

13
00:01:23.874 --> 00:01:36.844
Command injection patterns often hide in convenience helpers: generic run-command functions, remote command wrappers, string templates, or logging code that later gets reused for execution.

14
00:01:36.844 --> 00:01:45.412
During review, ask whether any part of a command line came from parameters, files, environment variables, or service responses.

15
00:01:45.412 --> 00:01:53.430
If it did, prefer structured invocation, allowlists, explicit mapping, and narrow values over free-form command text.
